All 55 tests pass. 

## Summary

The change is implemented in `AssignCustomerCode.java`. Previously it set the customer code to the pure `<REGION>-<HASH8>` value from `CustomerCode.of(owner)` with no collision handling.

Now the `service` method also injects `OwnerRepository`, collects every existing owner's `customerCode`, and — if the computed base code already exists — appends `-<n>` starting at `n = 2`, incrementing until the code is unique:

```java
String code = base;
for (int n = 2; existing.contains(code); n++) {
    code = base + "-" + n;
}
owner.setCustomerCode(code);
```

This step runs before `SaveOwner` (per the `owners.POST.yml` pipeline), so the comparison naturally excludes the owner being created and only considers already-persisted owners, matching the same convention used by `AssignNamesakeCount`. The de-duplicated `customerCode` flows through to the response.

The spec's collision path can't be forced through the black-box API (an identical hash needs an identical telephone+lastName, which telephone-uniqueness already rejects), so the new `Cp41Tests` asserts the guarantee — distinct owners get distinct codes — which passes, along with the full 55-test suite.

